.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt MINIXFS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm minixfs
.Nd format of MINIX file systems
.Sh DESCRIPTION
MINIX has had three versions of its file system, V1, V2 and V3.
Linux added to V1 and V2 names of 30 characters and a state field in the
super block, and Minix-vmd flags in the super block and flex
directories, whose entries are as long as their names.
The tools support both byte orders.
See the command manuals for supported operations and restrictions.
.Ss Layout
A file system is a sequence of blocks, of 1024 bytes in V1 and V2 and of
the size its super block gives in V3:
.Bl -column "block 2 + imap blocks" "Contents" -offset indent
.It Sy Block Ta Sy Contents
.It 0 Ta boot block
.It 1 Ta super block, at byte 1024 in every version
.It 2 Ta inode map, imap blocks
.It 2 + imap blocks Ta zone map, zmap blocks
.It after the maps Ta inode table
.It first data zone Ta data zones, to the end
.El
.Pp
Data is kept in zones of 2 to the power of the zone size blocks.
Zones are numbered from the start of the file system, and the numbers
of the zones before the first data zone are never used in files.
.Ss Super block
All numbers are stored in the byte order of the machine that made the
file system: little-endian on the PC, big-endian on the 68000 machines
(Atari ST, Amiga, Macintosh).
The magic number tells the version, the name length and the byte order:
.Bl -column "0x138f" "Version" "Names" -offset indent
.It Sy Magic Ta Sy Version Ta Sy Names Ta Sy Origin
.It 0x137f Ta V1 Ta 14 Ta MINIX
.It 0x137f Ta V1 Ta 14, 60 Ta Minix-vmd
.It 0x138f Ta V1 Ta 30 Ta Linux
.It 0x2468 Ta V2 Ta 14 Ta MINIX
.It 0x2468 Ta V2 Ta 14, 60 Ta Minix-vmd
.It 0x2478 Ta V2 Ta 30 Ta Linux
.It 0x4d5a Ta V3 Ta 60 Ta MINIX 3
.El
.Pp
V1 and V2 share one super block, which holds, at these byte offsets:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta inodes
.It 2 Ta 2 Ta zones (V1)
.It 4 Ta 2 Ta inode map blocks
.It 6 Ta 2 Ta zone map blocks
.It 8 Ta 2 Ta first data zone
.It 10 Ta 2 Ta log2 of blocks in a zone
.It 12 Ta 4 Ta maximum file size
.It 16 Ta 2 Ta magic number
.It 18 Ta 2 Ta 0
.It 20 Ta 4 Ta zones (V2)
.El
.Pp
Linux and Minix-vmd use this super block too, and differ from it here:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Linux keeps at 18 a state field, s_state: 1 cleanly unmounted, 2 errors
found.
.It
Minix-vmd keeps the zone size in byte 10 alone and flags in byte 11,
0x01 for flex directories and 0x02 for clean, and 0x7f, 0x13 at 18
where its clean flag is enabled; see
.Sx Minix-vmd .
.El
.Pp
The super block of V3 has a layout of its own:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 4 Ta inodes
.It 6 Ta 2 Ta inode map blocks
.It 8 Ta 2 Ta zone map blocks
.It 10 Ta 2 Ta first data zone, or 0 if it does not fit; it then follows the inode table
.It 12 Ta 2 Ta log2 of blocks in a zone
.It 14 Ta 2 Ta flags: 0x0001 clean; the bits of 0xff00 must be known to mount
.It 16 Ta 4 Ta maximum file size
.It 20 Ta 4 Ta zones
.It 24 Ta 2 Ta magic number
.It 28 Ta 2 Ta block size
.El
.Pp
MINIX 3 mounts a file system that is not marked clean read-only.
The maximum file size limits files; MINIX works it out from what the
double indirect zone reaches, while Linux writes 2147483647 in V2 and V3.
.Ss Bit maps
There are two bit maps, right after the super block: the inode map
(imap), of as many blocks as the super block gives for it, then the zone
map (zmap).
Bit
.Em i
of the inode map is set when inode
.Em i
is in use; bit
.Em i
of the zone map when zone first data zone +
.Em i
\- 1 is.
Bit 0 of both is never used and is always set.
The maps are arrays of words of 16 bits in V1 and V2 and of 32 bits in
V3, in the byte order of the file system, bit 0 of a word being its
lowest.
The bits past the last inode or zone are left clear by the mkfs of MINIX
and set by that of Linux.
.Ss Inodes
Inode 1 is the root directory; inode 0 does not exist.
A V1 inode is 32 bytes:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta mode: type and permission bits
.It 2 Ta 2 Ta owner
.It 4 Ta 4 Ta size
.It 8 Ta 4 Ta modification time
.It 12 Ta 1 Ta group
.It 13 Ta 1 Ta link count
.It 14 Ta 9 \(mu 2 Ta zones: 7 direct, single and double indirect
.El
.Pp
A V2 and V3 inode is 64 bytes:
.Bl -column "Offset" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta mode
.It 2 Ta 2 Ta link count
.It 4 Ta 2 Ta owner
.It 6 Ta 2 Ta group
.It 8 Ta 4 Ta size
.It 12 Ta 4 Ta access time
.It 16 Ta 4 Ta modification time
.It 20 Ta 4 Ta status change time
.It 24 Ta 10 \(mu 4 Ta zones: 7 direct, single, double and triple indirect
.El
.Pp
Times are seconds since the Epoch.
The types in the mode are those of
.Xr stat 2 :
0100000 regular file, 0040000 directory, 0120000 symbolic link, 0020000
character device, 0060000 block device, 0010000 pipe, 0140000 socket.
A free inode has the mode 0.
.Pp
Zone 0 in a zone slot is a hole, which reads as zeros.
An indirect zone lists zone numbers of 16 bits in V1 and 32 bits in V2
and V3, in its first block.
MINIX never uses the triple indirect zone, and follows only the zones
the double indirect zone reaches; Linux and this package use it too.
Without it a file can have up to these sizes, which the mkfs of MINIX
writes as the maximum file size; zones of 2 to the power
.Em k
blocks reach 2 to the power
.Em k
times as far, up to 2147483647:
.Bl -column "V2, V3 (1024-byte blocks)" -offset indent
.It Sy Kind Ta Sy Bytes
.It V1 Ta 268966912
.It V2, V3 (1024-byte blocks) Ta 67378176
.It V3 (2048-byte blocks) Ta 537933824
.It V3 (4096 bytes and larger) Ta 2147483647
.El
.Pp
A device keeps its device number, major times 256 plus minor, in its
first zone slot.
A symbolic link keeps its target in its data, without a NUL.
.Ss Directories
A directory is a file of entries.
The first entry is
.Ql \&.
and the second
.Ql \&.. ,
which in the root is the root itself.
The kind of entry follows from the magic number and, in Minix-vmd, from
the flex flag, 0x01 in byte 11 of the super block.
Offsets are in bytes from the start of the entry:
.Bl -column "Minix-vmd flex" "0x137f, 0x2468" "Size" "0-1" -offset indent
.It Sy Kind Ta Sy Magic Ta Sy Size Ta Sy Inode Ta Sy Name
.It MINIX Ta 0x137f, 0x2468 Ta 16 Ta 0\(en1 Ta 2\(en15
.It Linux Ta 0x138f, 0x2478 Ta 32 Ta 0\(en1 Ta 2\(en31
.It MINIX 3 Ta 0x4d5a Ta 64 Ta 0\(en3 Ta 4\(en63
.It Minix-vmd flex Ta 0x137f, 0x2468 Ta 8\(en64 Ta 0\(en1 Ta 3\(enend
.El
.Pp
In MINIX, Linux and MINIX 3, every entry is of the size in the table.
The name is padded with NUL bytes to the end of its field, and has no
NUL when it fills the field.
.Pp
A flex entry is 1 to 8 slots of 8 bytes:
.Bl -column "8 to end" "Bytes" -offset indent
.It Sy Offset Ta Sy Bytes Ta Sy Field
.It 0 Ta 2 Ta inode number
.It 2 Ta 1 Ta number of slots after the first, 0 to 7
.It 3 Ta 5 Ta the first 5 bytes of the name
.It 8 to end Ta 8 each Ta the rest of the name, then NUL bytes
.El
.Pp
A name of
.Em n
characters takes 1 +
.No \&( Ns Em n No + 3) / 8
slots, the division rounded down, which leaves room for at least one
NUL, and is padded with NUL bytes to the end of the last slot.
An entry lies within one block.
.Pp
An entry whose inode number is 0 is free.
Minix-vmd frees a flex entry by setting bytes 0 to 2 of each of its
slots to 0, and adds an entry at the first run of free slots in one
block that is long enough, or else in a new block.
.Ss Minix-vmd
Minix-vmd, the MINIX derivative of Philip Homburg and Kees Bot, writes V1
and V2 with the magic numbers of MINIX.
Its super block and flex directories are described above.
Its clean flag is kept on the disk only where bytes 18 and 19 of the
super block hold 0x7f and 0x13: its mkfs writes them for flex
directories, and
.Ql fsck -c
writes or clears them.
The tools of this package know Minix-vmd by these bytes; a file system
without them is read as one of MINIX.
.Ss Systems
The systems read and write these kinds:
.Bl -column "MINIX 3 after 3.3.0" -offset indent
.It Sy System Ta Sy Kinds
.It MINIX 1.0 to 1.5 Ta MINIX V1
.It MINIX 1.6.23 to 2.0.4 Ta MINIX V1, V2
.It Minix-vmd 1.7.0 Ta MINIX V1, V2, and flex directories
.It MINIX 3 to 3.2.1 Ta MINIX V1, V2, V3
.It MINIX 3.3.0 Ta V3
.It MINIX 3 after 3.3.0 Ta V3 with zones of one block
.It Linux Ta MINIX and Linux V1, V2; V3 from 2.6.21
.It this package Ta all, but flex directories are only read
.El
.Pp
The extension of Linux, names of 30 characters, is thus read and
written only by Linux and by this package.
MINIX 3 after 3.3.0 refuses zones of more than one block because its MFS
has come to handle zones as blocks; the check, added in 2015, keeps it
from failing later instead.
MINIX from 1.6.23 reads V1 and V2 in either byte order; Linux, and
MINIX 3 for V3, only in that of the machine.
Linux takes V3 blocks only up to the page size, 4096 on the PC.
The mkfs of MINIX 3 makes V3 only from 3.2.1; that of Linux, mkfs.minix
of util-linux, makes V2 from 1994 and V3 from 2.20.
.Ss Images of one side of a disk
A disk written on one side and read as double-sided, as some of the
Atari ST, holds the file system in every other track of the image.
The option
.Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
of the tools reads such an image; see
.Xr minixfs 1 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr dump_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr mount_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr restore_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr tunefs_minixfs 8
.Rs
.%A Andrew S. Tanenbaum
.%A Albert S. Woodhull
.%B Operating Systems: Design and Implementation
.%O second and third editions
.Re
.Sh HISTORY
.Bl -tag -width "1991"
.It 1987
V1 is the file system of MINIX 1.0, of the first edition of the book.
.It 1991
Linux uses V1 from its first release, 0.01.
MINIX 1.5 for the Atari ST, the Amiga and the Macintosh, 68000 ports
that write V1 big-endian, is distributed.
.It 1992
V2, with 32-bit zone numbers and the times of access and status change,
is in MINIX 1.6.23, which also takes a file system made on a machine of
the other byte order, whose magic number then reads with its bytes
swapped, 0x7f13 for 0x137f.
.It 1993
Linux adds 30-character names, magic 0x138f, by 0.99.7, and the state
field by 0.99.15.
.It 1994
mkfs.minix of Linux makes V2.
.It 1996
Linux reads V2, and V2 with 30-character names, magic 0x2478, by 2.0,
and uses the triple indirect zone that MINIX leaves unused.
Minix-vmd 1.7.0, based on MINIX 1.7, has the flags in its super block
and flex directories; no later release of Minix-vmd was published.
.It 1997
V2 is the file system of MINIX 2.0, of the second edition of the book.
.It 2005
V3, with blocks of other sizes and 60-character names, is the file
system of MINIX 3.
.It 2007
Linux reads V3 from 2.6.21.
.It 2010
MINIX 3.1.6 writes 0 as the first data zone of V3 where it does not fit
in 16 bits, and takes it to follow the inode table.
.It 2011
mkfs.minix of util-linux makes V3 from 2.20.
.It 2012
MINIX 3.2.0 keeps a clean flag in the flags of V3.
.It 2013
The mkfs of MINIX 3.2.1 makes V3 only, where that of 3.0 to 3.2.0 also
made V1 and V2.
.It 2014
MINIX 3.3.0 reads V3 only.
.It 2015
MINIX 3 after 3.3.0 refuses zones of more than one block.
.El
.Sh CAVEATS
MINIX reads the part of a file past what the double indirect zone
reaches as zeros, without an error.
In V2 and V3, Linux and this package write past it, through the triple
indirect zone, up to the maximum file size in the super block, which
mkfs.minix of Linux and
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8
set to 2147483647.
A file over 67378176 bytes in V2 with 1024-byte blocks is thus not read
in full by MINIX.
.Ql tunefs_minixfs -m minix
keeps both to what MINIX reads.
.Pp
MINIX 3 after 3.3.0 does not mount a file system whose zones are of more
than one block, as
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8
.Fl z
makes.
.Sh BUGS
A file system of Minix-vmd with flex directories whose clean flag was
disabled with
.Ql fsck -c
lacks the bytes 0x7f and 0x13, and is refused as not a MINIX file
system: read as one of MINIX, its flags make the zone size too large.
